cartridge "TRAP/Eric Haines/1988"

' TRAP
' A ColecoVision game based on "Trap" by Eric Haines, COMPUTE!'s Gazette
' May 1988 pp. 26-27 (Commodore 64, machine language, SYS 2061). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (from the article
' and a disassembly of the machine code, orig/trap.dis, timed in jsc64),
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: tiled 3-D blocks over a twinkling
' starfield, a sphere sprite that bounces in smooth arcs with a shadow,
' sound effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: sixteen coloured blocks (4 x 4) hang in space. You are the
' blinking flat square; the sphere bounces from block to block, on its own,
' up, down, left or right, never off the grid. You leave a snake-like
' trail of four discoloured blocks (your own block and the three behind
' you); the sphere cannot take off toward a discoloured block (if it is
' already in the air it still lands where it was going), and you cannot
' move onto your trail. Trap the sphere where all four sides are
' discoloured blocks or the void: one point for every unit left on the time
' bar, then the next screen, in new colours. Every third screen the sphere
' is faster, up to a limit. When the time bar runs out you take a shock
' (two are shown at the top); the third ends the game.
'
' From the original (numbers from the machine code): the 4 x 4 grid, the
' start (square bottom right with its trail along the bottom row, sphere
' second row, second column), the trail of four that only moves when you
' do (so a square boxed in by its own trail is helpless until time runs
' out), the sphere's random direction among the free sides, the trap test
' made when the sphere is about to take off, 280 time units at one unit
' per 42 ticks (POKE 3258), 16 ticks a frame (the main delay of 170,
' POKE 2910, measured in jsc64: a sphere hop of 560 ticks = 35 frames), a
' hop of 56 steps at one step per 10 ticks at first, one tick less every
' three screens down to 3 (the 2 of POKE 4211), the square's move of 32
' steps at 9 ticks, a point per time unit left, seven colour sets, two
' shocks survived, the high score.
'
' Liberties: the grid is drawn 5 tiles across a block (the C64's 7) to fit
' 28 columns; the sphere flies in a smooth arc with a shadow (the C64's path
' was three straight legs); a diagonal on the stick takes its up/down part,
' or its left/right part when that way is blocked (the C64 ignored
' diagonals); the trail blocks are dithered as well as recoloured; the
' square blinks at 2 Hz and the stars twinkle slowly (the C64's star colours
' changed about ten times a second); the shock and the end pulse at 2.5 Hz
' (the C64 flashed faster); no RUN/STOP pause (joystick only); a press
' after the end goes back to the title.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick moves the square one block. On
' the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text and tiles in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code
' 128; the frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the
' NMI off, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the
' title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Nothing
' flashes faster than 3 Hz.

